QA audit by qa-b2df68b9, 2026-08-09 ~15:32Z. Approving.

## Independence disclosure, first, because I am not arms-length on this task

**I co-authored the ruling that created it.** This task's description cites `msg-1202c09b` — mine — as one of the two converged QA rulings it was split out on. I also routed @worker-4addc779's `dist/` finding here at 15:03Z. I did not specify any assertion in the file, but I helped decide that this task should exist and what it should carry, so I verified everything by **mutation and measurement rather than by reading**, and I am recording the conflict here rather than leaving it in the channel.

**A claim of mine was overtaken and I am correcting it rather than letting it stand.** At 15:03Z I told the channel the two new surfaces had "zero rendered parity coverage today". That was true of the tree I measured — but it is false now, and the architect's step-1 inversion is right. Measured:
```
git show 16bd8e5^:<file> | grep -c TABLE_SURFACES  ->  0
git show 16bd8e5:<file>  | grep -c TABLE_SURFACES  ->  6
```
The parity block was not in committed bytes before this commit. It is now. Whoever reads this task should treat the architect's measurement as governing and my 15:03 statement as superseded.

## Gate re-run by me

`pnpm --filter @moe/control-room test` -> **39 files / 569 tests, exit 0.** The recorded command is the DoD's own with no narrowing.

## The 79 foreign lines — I reviewed them, because otherwise nobody would

The worker disclosed that `16bd8e5` is 141 insertions of which only ~62 are theirs, the rest being task-ddb3bf77's uncommitted `TABLE_SURFACES` block that could not be separated without interactive staging. **Verified:** filtering the `-U0` diff for non-comment additions returns exactly that block — imports, `RunLeaseProjection`/`ResourceProjection` fixtures, `TABLE_SURFACES`, and its describe. The worker's own change really is comment-only.

The consequence they flagged is the important part, and it is sharper than they put it: **those 79 lines have never been through QA.** @qa-58b24ffb approved task-ddb3bf77 at 14:46Z against a file that did not contain them, and that task is DONE, so no future pass covers them either. Reviewing them here:

```ts
it("sweeps both table surfaces, each generated with its affordances intact", () => {
  expect(TABLE_SURFACES.length).toBe(2);
  expect(RUN_COMMANDS).toHaveLength(1);
  expect(RESOURCE_COMMANDS).toHaveLength(1);
  const swept = new Set(SURFACES.map((f) => f.id));
  expect(TABLE_SURFACES.filter((f) => swept.has(f.id))).toEqual([]);
});
it.each(...)("renders the same field set wide and narrow on %s", (_id, fixture) => {
  const wide = surfaceTestIds(mountAt(fixture, WIDE));
  cleanup();
  const narrow = surfaceTestIds(mountAt(fixture, NARROW));
  expect(wide.size).toBeGreaterThan(0);
  expect(narrow.size).toBeGreaterThan(0);
  expect(sorted(narrow)).toEqual(sorted(wide));
});
```
**This is @governor-f70d1157's 14:39:08 ordered shape, exactly** — "a real parity assertion over the new runs/resources surfaces... assert both narrow and wide id sets are NON-EMPTY before comparing." The order is satisfied; it simply landed under this task's commit instead of task-ddb3bf77's. The exclusion assertion also proves the two sweeps cannot overlap, which is why the 15-count neither moves nor needs to.

## DoD coverage

**1 — spec at source.** Quoted verbatim with file digest and line number: section 4.16 line 432, *"Tables drop to two-line rows; no column removed, only reflowed."* Split into (a) field-set, mechanically checkable, covered; (b) two-line rows, a pixel property jsdom cannot reach. The digest in the note matches task rail 1 character for character.

**2 — parity coverage.** Already satisfied on disk and correctly not duplicated. Building a second sweep would have been the stale-by-default rail's gap-claimed-present-actually-closed direction — two blocks asserting one property, one of which diverges later.

**3 — the unchanged 15 is the correct outcome.** The surfaces were never added to `CORE_`/`OPS_SURFACE_FIXTURES`, so the by-literal-name clause is vacuous. I checked this rather than accepting the reasoning: the fixture files are untouched and the exclusion assertion holds.

**4 — drills.** Three recorded. I re-ran the one they nominated (below).

**5 — the dangling pointer is gone.** `grep -c bc0b8f5b` on the file returns **0**. This is the item I would have caught if it had been missed, since a committed comment naming a *closed* task as the pending owner is exactly the class of silently-stale claim this whole chain started from.

**6 — gate exit 0**, reproduced.

## Drill run by me, not read

Removed the `resources-surface` entry from `TABLE_SURFACES` (backup outside the repo, atomic restore):
```
expect(TABLE_SURFACES.length).toBe(2);   ->  1 failed
Tests  1 failed | 20 passed (21)
```
**21 tests, not 22.** The resources parity row did not fail — it **stopped existing**, and every assertion inside it "passed" by never running. The only thing that caught the loss was the count guard. That is epic rail 6's zero-case vacuity demonstrated on this exact file rather than quoted at it: without `expect(TABLE_SURFACES.length).toBe(2)`, deleting a table surface would be an invisible, green removal of its coverage.

Restore verified by `sha256` — `59a0e4c8…c59b7ebb` both sides, MATCH. `git log -1` still `16bd8e5` (no completion hook swept the window). `git rev-parse HEAD:<file>` == `git hash-object <file>` — `4b32256d…` MATCH. Drill hygiene from the worker's own drills confirmed too: `apps/control-room/src/a11y/`, `runs/` and `resources/` are all clean, and grep finds no `drill-sixteenth` or `innerWidth >= 960` residue.

## The one thing I routed here that is NOT satisfied — and is not a rejection reason

At 15:03 I named @worker-4addc779's finding as an obligation this task should carry: the presence scan's `SOURCE_ROOT` is `join(dirname(THIS_FILE), "..")` over a bare recursive `readdirSync` with no ignore list, so one hop shallower puts `dist/` in scope and the built bundle carries `cr.runs`/`cr.resources` as string literals **after the source surface is deleted**. `expect(scanned.length - files.length).toBe(1)` cannot see it — that guard proves containment, which survives the root growing.

**It is not in this task's DoD**, and I am not rejecting for it. I said "should carry" in a chat message; nobody wrote it into the DoD, and a QA agent does not get to enforce an obligation that exists only in their own channel post — that is precisely the failure mode I would reject a worker for. It needs its own task. Recording it here so the third mention is attached to something durable instead of scrolling away.
